test-hypotheses.prompt:
Incident {{state.incident}}. Triage (with any similar past incidents): {{state.triage}}

The evidence:

Metrics: {{state.evidence-metrics}}

Logs: {{state.evidence-logs}}

Recent changes: {{state.evidence-changes}}

Hosts: {{state.evidence-hosts}}

Traces and dependencies: {{state.evidence-traces}}

Kubernetes: {{state.evidence-k8s}}

A refuter's verdict on an earlier root cause, if any (its new evidence must be explained this time): {{state.refute}}

Form 2-4 hypotheses that could explain the symptom (a similar past incident's cause is one candidate, never assumed). For each, name the test that would confirm or refute it, RUN that test (read-only: queries, logs, probes, the traces and topology tools if they are available, and ssh_exec as the host's read-only account - the default; never as: "operator"), and record the result in the incident timeline (kind hypothesis) with the evidence ids. Do not change any system.

Report: each hypothesis, its test, and the result with numbers and evidence ids ([ev-...]).

refute.prompt:
Incident {{state.incident}}. The leading root cause:
{{state.root_cause}}
Its confidence: {{state.confidence}}
The evidence it cites: {{state.evidence_ids}}
The citation check: {{state.rca-cited}}

The hypotheses that were tested:
{{state.hypotheses}}

Try to FALSIFY it before anyone changes a system. Run 2-4 NEW read-only checks whose result would differ if it were wrong - do not just re-read the cited results:
- timing: did the symptom start before the supposed cause (prometheus_query around the start; change_timeline if available)?
- scope: is something affected that the cause cannot touch, or is something the cause should touch unaffected?
- mechanism: does the cause explain the numbers (dose and size), and do the logs (or trace data, where the traces tool is available) show the mechanism at the failing hop?
- alternatives: does something the service depends on fail deeper (topology dependencies, if available), or did another change happen at the same time?
Read each cited id with evidence show first. Cite the evidence id of each check you run. Record your verdict in the incident timeline (incident update, kind hypothesis, starting "Refuter:").

Report: each check, its result with numbers and evidence ids, and your verdict: SURVIVES when every check is consistent with the root cause (say what would still change your mind), REJECTED when a check contradicts it (say exactly which, with the numbers and ids, and what the hypotheses must explain next).

unconfirmed.message:
Incident {{state.incident}}: the refuter rejected the root cause {{state.refutations}} time(s); no root cause has survived.

Latest root cause: {{state.root_cause}}
Confidence: {{state.confidence}}

The refuter's verdict:
{{state.refute}}

Approve to write a remediation runbook for this root cause anyway (it is still checked and approved before anything runs), or reject to stop here and hand the incident over with the evidence gathered.

runbook.prompt:
Incident {{state.incident}}. Root cause: {{state.root_cause}}
Earlier feedback (a rejected runbook, a failed gate or verification), if any: {{feedback}}

Write the remediation as a runbook at .harness/ops/runbooks/remediate-{{state.incident}}.md following the runbook-writing skill: baseline [check] steps, an [approval] before each [change], exact commands or tool calls in backticks, a [verify] after each change with the expected numbers, and a Rollback section. A [change] on a host runs ssh_exec with as: "operator" (write it so: `ssh_exec(host="...", as="operator", command="...")`); [check] and [verify] steps run as the read-only account. A [change] on Kubernetes is the exact k8s_control call if it is available (e.g. `k8s_control(action="rollback", service="checkout")`); its [verify] is k8s_health for the service (rollout complete, N/N available). Preconditions must be checkable with the tools by whoever executes it; what only a person can decide (timing, an out-of-window change, an owner's confirmation) goes into the [approval] step - the person approving this runbook decides it. The smallest change that removes the root cause, using the tools and commands you found on the systems; prefer one inside a maintenance window, and say in the approval step when it is outside one. If topology is available, name the services the change can affect (topology dependents) in the approval step. Check it with the runbook tool (action check). Do NOT run any change now.

Report: the full runbook text (it is shown to the person who approves it).

approve.message:
Incident {{state.incident}}: approve running this remediation on the systems named in it, or reject with what to change.

Root cause: {{state.root_cause}}
Confidence: {{state.confidence}}
Evidence: {{state.evidence_ids}}
Refuter: {{state.refute}}

{{state.runbook}}

postmortem.prompt:
Incident {{state.incident}} is mitigated. Root cause: {{state.root_cause}}
Confidence: {{state.confidence}}. Evidence: {{state.evidence_ids}}. Refuter: {{state.refute}}
Confirm with ops_check and probe that the service is healthy now. Resolve the incident (incident resolve: root_cause, resolution), then write the postmortem with incident postmortem following the postmortem skill: summary, impact with numbers, detection, resolution, contributing factors, what went well/wrong, where we got lucky, action items (prevent, detect, mitigate - each with an owner role and priority), and the evidence: for each cited id (evidence show), the query or command, its numbers and its links (Prometheus, Grafana dashboards and panels, trace views); state the confidence with its reasons and whether the refuter's checks held.
If the grafana tool is available, mark the incident on Grafana: one annotation from its start to its resolution (time, time_end) tagged incident and {{state.incident}}, text = the title and root cause. It is asked about; if it is refused or fails, carry on.

Report: the postmortem's summary and its root cause line.

postmortem-unresolved.prompt:
Incident {{state.incident}} is NOT resolved: the remediation did not clear the alerts, or no root cause survived the refuter.
{{feedback}}
Refuter: {{state.refute}}
Leave the incident open (status investigating). Write a draft postmortem with incident postmortem saying plainly that it is unresolved, what was tried and what the evidence says (each with its evidence id and links), with action items for the next responder.

Report: the current state and the next steps.
